    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d376">

                <pb facs="0580" n="492" xml:id="pg492"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Wharton to Mr. Grubb.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1892-03-24"><hi rendition="#italic">March 24,
                        1892</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 198.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have received your No. 190, of January 16
                    last, indorsing copy of the Duke of Tetuan’s note of January 11, stating the
                    views of the Spanish Government in regard to the destruction of the mission
                    property at Ponape.</p>
                <p>Setting aside the arguments advanced to disclaim the responsibility of the
                    Spanish Government in this regard, and for the deportation of Mr. Doane, the
                    purport of the note seems to be that the reëstablishment of the mission will be
                    permitted, and that on receipt of advices still awaited, the question of
                    personal indemnification of Mr. Doane will be adjusted on satisfactory
                    terms.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">William F. Wharton</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Acting Secretary.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
